WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140 line:1
Good to be with you.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:01.940 line:1
I'm Tony Dokoupil.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:05.000 line:1
The breaking news right now.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:19.720 line:1
Double trouble-- millions bracing for dangerous weather on both coasts as a nor'easter could slam the East Coast with tropical-storm-force winds and heavy rain as Hurricane Polo spins out there in the Pacific, turning into one of the strongest storms ever.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.480 line:1
Rob Marciano in the storm zone.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.480 line:1
Also developing tonight President Trump's highly anticipated summit with China's Xi Jinping.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:38.100 line:1
Another military fighter jet crashes during a training mission-- the condition of the pilots tonight, the crew safely ejecting before it slams to the ground.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.920 line:1
What went wrong?

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:48.740 line:1
Bombshell testimony at the murder trial of that former American Idol contestant accused of killing his wife in their bed while their children slept nearby.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.680 line:1
What his former girlfriend said on the stand today.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:00.840 line:1
And why Dolly Parton's manager has filed for a restraining order against the man who announced her death just weeks ago.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:09.080 line:1
Rollercoaster lawsuits-- more than a hundred people say they suffered traumatic brain injuries from this California thrill ride.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.640 line:1
A close call on a Utah freeway, lightning nearly striking this truck.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.000 line:1
And the good stuff, the historic home run ball and the birthday girl, who threw it back.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.680 line:1
COMMENTATOR: And throwing it back.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.580 line:1
Oh my goodness.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:32.390 line:1
ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News with Tony Dokoupil.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.020 line:1
Good evening.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.980 line:1
Great to have you with us on a Wednesday night.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.720 line:1
We begin with dual updates on a series of major storms off both coasts.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:44.380 line:1
In the Atlantic, high winds already whipping up the surf ahead of what is now expected to become a nor'easter.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:58.500 line:1
Coastal flood alerts are already in place from the Carolinas up to the Jersey Shore ahead of landfall this weekend, while over in the Pacific, Hurricane Polo, a Category 4 storm, is expected to impact the California coast in the coming days.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:08.480 line:1
And for Hawaii's Big Island, forecasters are warning of the potential for catastrophic flooding and mudslides from what's expected to become another major hurricane.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.260 line:1
Rob Marciano tracking it all, as always, starting with the conditions off the East Coast right now.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.070 line:1
Rob, how's it looking?

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:22.500 line:1
Hey, Tony, well, pretty much the same it's been all day, which is a relentlessly consistent barrage of wind and water.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.620 line:1
I mean, look at that ocean.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.660 line:1
It just looks like an absolute washing machine.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:32.140 line:1
It's been pummeling this coastline all day long in Virginia, North Carolina, where they lost a lot of real estate with the storms last year.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.120 line:1
And this coastal storm is now just beginning to strengthen.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:47.180 line:1
Pounding surf pummeled Virginia Beach today, powered by winds reaching 45 miles per hour, bending trees, and whipping through this nearly deserted beach town.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.900 line:1
Nearby Norfolk, home to the largest US Naval base in the world, they're dealing with storm tide flooding.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.980 line:1
A lot of the floodgates here in Norfolk have been closed out ahead of this storm.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:06.360 line:1
For the water that does actually seep into the city, that's one of 14 pumping stations that they have to get water back out into the river.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.020 line:1
This slow-moving storm is expected to pack a one-two punch as it churns up the coast.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:17.380 line:1
Strong winds and full moon tides have the potential to bring coastal flooding and severe beach erosion.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.300 line:1
That's good.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:18.900 line:1
Go ahead.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.180 line:1
ROB MARCIANO: Residents in Massachusetts are preparing for the onslaught.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.620 line:1
Sometimes these storms take a turn for the worst.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:35.240 line:1
ROB MARCIANO: Meanwhile, several states along the Pacific are on high alert as Hurricane Polo spins, one of the strongest ever recorded, fueled by the epic El Niño.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:46.280 line:1
This was the view from inside the eye of this now Category 4 storm, part of its moisture streaming into the Southwest, now causing dangerous inland flooding in New Mexico.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:51.720 line:1
Back in Virginia Beach, Trey Coates and his family are in town for this weekend's Neptune Festival.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.820 line:1
They say they're not going anywhere.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.140 line:1
We've been battling the conditions, honestly.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:02.200 line:1
I had to hold the door open, almost flew-- I got sand all over my face.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:10.620 line:1
And just as we speak, the last few minutes, some of those vendors from that festival for the weekend beginning to build their structures, hoping it will hold on to this wind or this wind will die.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.380 line:1
But it shows no signs of doing that.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.460 line:1
The water now coming up and covering this beach.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.980 line:1
And that's going to be the calling card, at least here in the Mid-Atlantic.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.780 line:1
And that is coastal flooding all the way up to Delmarva, into New Jersey, and potentially much of the Northeast as well.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:28.100 line:1
That on top of waves that could be 6 to 12 feet, winds 50 miles an hour or better.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.570 line:1
And there, you see your warnings.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:39.580 line:1
As far as what we think this storm is going to do-- it will strengthen and expand over the next 24 hours, then ride parallel up the Jersey coastline, impacting Long Island and then New England.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.660 line:1
Rain, yes, a big surge of water, and wind that could be damaging.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:49.180 line:1
But if it takes the other model, the GFS solution, it would be a lesser blow, at least to New York City.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.340 line:1
We're certainly hoping for that.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.420 line:1
But Eastern New England looks to be getting it.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.660 line:1
Polo, big-time storm in the Pacific.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.420 line:1
But now we have Nolo.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:04.580 line:1
And that is going to threaten Hawaii, not just that, probably hit the Big Island over the weekend with potentially Category 2 or 3 winds.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:08.860 line:1
And, Tony, they are still recovering from a big hurricane just a little over a month ago.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.150 line:1
Not what they need in Hawaii.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:10.860 line:1
No, not at all.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.400 line:1
Rob, thank you very much.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.500 line:1
Now to something that hasn't happened in more than a decade-- an official state visit to the White House from Chinese leader, Xi Jinping.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.770 line:1
And there is much for the two leaders to cover, from AI regulation to the wars in Ukraine and the Middle East.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.180 line:1
But how much of it we're going to see-- that is an open question tonight.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.410 line:1
Nancy Cordes has the latest.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:38.040 line:1
NANCY CORDES: Not content to wait at the White House, President Trump choppered to Joint Base Andrews this afternoon to greet Chinese President Xi Jinping.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:45.240 line:1
It's the first time a US president has made that gesture for a foreign head of state since 1962.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.740 line:1
We're doing very well with China.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.520 line:1
And President Xi and I have a good relationship.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.000 line:1
NANCY CORDES: The US and China are at odds over AI, trade, and most importantly, Iran.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.080 line:1
Trump, stop fawning over strongmen.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.460 line:1
Stop calling Xi a great gentleman.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:06.630 line:1
NANCY CORDES: Lawmakers from both sides urged Trump to dial back the warm welcome.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:11.670 line:1
His guest is a brutal, unelected, and oppressive dictator.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:26.830 line:1
NANCY CORDES: The two men are set to sit down tomorrow, but video of the historic meeting may be sparse, as the five US TV networks continue to hold off on providing pooled coverage after Trump banned three major outlets from the White House.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:27.890 line:1
They're fake news.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:39.110 line:1
NANCY CORDES: In an emergency hearing this afternoon, Federal Judge Timothy Kelly said, quote, "I think it's fair to say that the process laid out for the White House in previous cases wasn't followed here."

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:44.150 line:1
I think the judge was very skeptical of the Trump administration's arguments here.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.890 line:1
NANCY CORDES: CBS News Legal Analyst Jessica Levinson.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:07:04.230 line:1
This is a case where there's a lot of evidence to indicate that the Trump administration is doing exactly what the First Amendment protects against, which is the government picking winners and losers based on who they like and whose speech they like and who they don't.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:14.570 line:1
NANCY CORDES: The judge is expected to make a decision within the next few days about whether to let these three outlets back onto White House grounds temporarily as the case plays out.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:15.070 line:1
Tony?

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.110 line:1
We'll keep covering it.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:16.870 line:1
Nancy, thank you.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:20.790 line:1
Now let's bring in Face the Nation moderator and chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.130 line:1
Margaret, great to have you here.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:27.510 line:1
So the president is rolling out the red carpet for the Chinese president, some say a little bit too much red carpet.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.770 line:1
But what are you expecting?

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:36.290 line:1
Well, Tony, whenever the two most powerful men in the world meet, there's typically a lot of picture-rich choreography.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.030 line:1
China's media state's controlled.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:38.830 line:1
They want this.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.270 line:1
But this time, it's different.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:44.680 line:1
The pictures won't be, as Nancy just told you, beamed around the world in the same way.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:57.130 line:1
And that's of concern to some of the Chinese officials I spoke with, in part because Xi Jinping lands here feeling he has the upper hand in the competition with the US, according to multiple Asian diplomats.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.230 line:1
Trump needs China to stop buying Iranian oil.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:04.250 line:1
He wants them to stop providing intelligence to Tehran.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:10.430 line:1
China has also cornered the market on a really critical set of ingredients for US-made weapons and technology.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:18.820 line:1
On the other hand, the question for Trump is whether he wants to extend, and for how long, the trade truce with China.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:21.910 line:1
And, of course, there are two Americans being wrongfully detained.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:23.230 line:1
You mentioned Iran there.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:30.230 line:1
Another extraordinary moment at the UN today-- the Iranian president here, at war with the US, and yet, here on US soil, giving a speech.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:31.650 line:1
What stood out to you?

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:37.550 line:1
Well, just a few months ago, he was in a bunker, sheltering from US airstrikes, and now he's standing in the middle of New York City.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.640 line:1
That, in and of itself, is extraordinary.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:45.310 line:1
Also extraordinary, President Trump's language yesterday when he said he wants to possibly annihilate Iran.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.170 line:1
This is an institution built to avoid war.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:52.470 line:1
But Iran's president took to the podium, and he had similar bluster.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.990 line:1
He said Iran will never surrender its civilian nuclear program.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:03.030 line:1
He also held up pictures of about what he said were 150 children killed there in airstrikes in the beginning of the war.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:07.630 line:1
But behind the scenes, I'm told both sides still are trying to find a way to negotiate.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.010 line:1
They said there was a good meeting, is how the president characterized it.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:10.750 line:1
Margaret, great to have you here.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:11.330 line:1
Appreciate it.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.050 line:1
And we'll see you this Sunday, of course, on Face the Nation.

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:21.390 line:1
And there's news tonight about the USS Lincoln strike group, which recently left the war zone in the Middle East after a marathon deployment there.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:25.330 line:1
We previously reported on concerns about the mental health of the sailors.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.590 line:1
And our Charlie D'Agata is here with an update.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:29.130 line:1
Charlie, good evening.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.070 line:1
Good evening, Tony.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:41.510 line:1
So, according to a letter from the Navy's acting secretary, there were eight suicide attempts by sailors aboard the USS Abraham Lincoln strike group during that long 10-month deployment.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.390 line:1
Now, that strike group includes multiple ships.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:49.230 line:1
And, notably, they were taking part in combat operations against Iran for much of that time.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:54.410 line:1
Now, as you said, you'll recall the Lincoln was plagued by supply issues during its deployment.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:10:02.210 line:1
Family members also raised the alarm about morale and mental health of the sailors because of that time on board without a break.

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:08.390 line:1
So in that letter, the acting Navy secretary wrote that no service members killed themselves during the deployment.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:08.890 line:1
Tony?

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:09.670 line:1
Well, that's good news.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.110 line:1
Charlie, thank you very much.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:16.270 line:1
For the third time now, all in less than a week, a military fighter jet has crashed during a training mission.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:21.390 line:1
Today it was a British Royal Air Force jet that went down in Wales, narrowly missing buildings there.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:25.290 line:1
And it follows recent crashes of two US F-16s.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.540 line:1
Leigh Kiniry is following this from our London Bureau.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:34.550 line:1
LEIGH KINIRY: Tony, you can see the Hawk 2 jet struggling to gain altitude in this video posted on social media.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.530 line:1
Moments later, the two pilots eject from the cockpit.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:43.550 line:1
And less than 20 seconds after that, the plane nosedives into a field, exploding into a fireball.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:45.990 line:1
It narrowly missed nearby homes.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:52.950 line:1
Witnesses said they noticed trouble from the start, describing fire coming from the jet, but the two airmen landed safely nearby.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:57.330 line:1
They were treated just for minor injuries, and no one else was hurt in the crash.

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:00.830 line:1
The Royal Air Force have not yet said what they believe went wrong.

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.390 line:1
But the Hawk T2 is used to train fighter pilots.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:06.530 line:1
It can reach speeds of up to 650 miles an hour.

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:12.010 line:1
Tonight, the Hawk jets are temporarily grounded while the Royal Air Force investigates this crash.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:12.700 line:1
Tony?

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:13.910 line:1
Leigh, thank you very much.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:18.970 line:1
We turn now to Ohio and the trial of a former American Idol contestant accused of murdering his wife.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:24.150 line:1
On the stand today, his former girlfriend-- and it did not seem to go well for the defense.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.410 line:1
CBS's Ian Lee has the update.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:34.530 line:1
IAN LEE: Today, 24-year-old Alleigha Botner, a former church intern and Caleb Flynn's one-time lover, took the stand.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.210 line:1
I hate him.

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:43.730 line:1
IAN LEE: Prosecutors had Botner read aloud some of the more than 100,000 messages between the two during their affair.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.750 line:1
At first, it was, I want her dead.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:46.790 line:1
I wish she was gone.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:51.810 line:1
And then it was, if I could kill her and not go to jail, I would.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:59.290 line:1
IAN LEE: The prosecution says Flynn's affair with Botner drove him to murder his wife, Ashley, a charge he denies.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:04.890 line:1
Prosecutors also showed a video Flynn sent to Botner, highlighting his obsession with her.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.930 line:1
CALEB FLYNN: Anybody who's listening, I love Aaliyah Botner.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:13.860 line:1
IAN LEE: On the stand, Botner recalled the moment she learned of Ashley Flynn's death.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.030 line:1
My heart started pounding.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:23.550 line:1
And in my mind, it seemed like too much of a coincidence that the day he's divorcing her, she's just dead, all of a sudden.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:35.450 line:1
IAN LEE: The former American Idol contestant and pastor is accused of shooting his wife twice while she slept in their Ohio home in February and attempted to cover up the killing by staging a burglary.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.470 line:1
A gun was never recovered.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:43.370 line:1
Investigators became suspicious of his claim by the lack of outside damage to a garage door.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:50.610 line:1
It wasn't what I would expect to see on a forced entry type of scenario.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:54.230 line:1
IAN LEE: Flynn claims he was in another room when his wife was murdered.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:13:03.470 line:1
But there are questions around why the couple's two dogs, known for being highly energetic, remains silent with an alleged intruder inside the house.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.090 line:1
Tony?

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:05.310 line:1
Ian, thank you.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:14.730 line:1
Dolly Parton's nephew, the one who informed all of us about her death, is now under a court order to stay out of the majority of the business dealings she left behind.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:18.550 line:1
Nicole Valdes is in Nashville with the new allegations.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:23.980 line:1
This video announcement is something Dolly asked of me years ago, before I fully absorbed it as a future reality.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:31.970 line:1
NICOLE VALDES: The man who told the world that Dolly Parton died, her nephew, Bryan Seaver, is now at the heart of a messy legal battle.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:44.850 line:1
Parton's longtime manager, Danny Nozell, and the LLC that manages many of Parton's businesses, claims Seaver, who headed Parton's security, had become, quote, "emboldened and relentless."

00:13:44.850 --> 00:14:06.200 line:1
The legal filings accuse him of trying to extort Parton's estate and making "terroristic threats, harassment, and intimidation" and sending threatening texts to Nozell and staff members, saying things like, "I'm going to destroy the entire brand," and "I'm going to set up a podcast dedicated to ruining Dolly's brand partnerships."

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:10.710 line:1
I mean, they literally call him a terrorist in this complaint.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:18.810 line:1
The text messages that they've provided do paint a picture, a very scary picture, of, really, an unhinged person.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:23.400 line:1
However, I will say that it's light on the technical legal details.

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:31.330 line:1
NICOLE VALDES: According to court documents, Nozell says Seaver was terminated last week and Parton's team cut ties with his security company.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:37.490 line:1
One of the threats Seaver's accused of making says, quote, "I'm not an entertainment person, I'm a killer."

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:45.050 line:1
Late today, a Nashville judge granted the restraining order Nozell and the LLC requested against Seaver.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:49.190 line:1
And CBS News has reached out to Dolly's estate, Nozell, and Seaver.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:50.510 line:1
None have responded.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:15:01.110 line:1
But according to multiple reports, including one from TMZ, Seaver denies the claims in this petition, saying his messages were taken out of context, that they were not threats.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:05.250 line:1
But he did add, quote, "Most of the comments, I stand by."

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:10.850 line:1
Meanwhile, Dolly's new hotel and museum here in Nashville are set to open next week.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:11.570 line:1
Tony?

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:12.070 line:1
All right.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:13.410 line:1
Nicole, thank you.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:21.470 line:1
Still ahead on the broadcast tonight, the dozens of people who claim they rode a Six Flags roller coaster and walked away with life-altering brain injuries.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:35.850 line:1
California's Six Flags Magic Mountain describes its X2 roller coaster as a ride for the ultimate daredevil and, quote, "downright scary."

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.270 line:1
But is it also dangerous?

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:42.370 line:1
Lawyers for more than a hundred people say the coaster left them with traumatic brain injuries.

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:46.530 line:1
Our Carter Evans reports.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:58.540 line:1
CARTER EVANS: Reaching speeds up to 76 miles per hour with seats that spin 360 degrees, the X2 is Six Flags Magic Mountain's signature roller coaster.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:04.080 line:1
But for Naomi Greer-Wilkinson, that thrill may have come at a devastating cost.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.980 line:1
When we got there, they told us to prepare for end of life.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:14.480 line:1
CARTER EVANS: Her parents allege that Naomi's ride on the X2 in July landed her in the hospital with a traumatic brain injury.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.400 line:1
She's still on a ventilator.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:23.430 line:1
Naomi had no idea that she was signing up for something that could potentially rip the veins in her skull.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:39.140 line:1
CARTER EVANS: The Dordick law corporation represents more than 100 people who say they've suffered traumatic brain injuries after riding the X2, including Michael Wilk, an attorney who said he underwent two brain surgeries to stop the internal bleeding.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.220 line:1
I struggle for words.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.850 line:1
I don't necessarily think clearly.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:51.880 line:1
CARTER EVANS: As a result of his injuries, Wilk says he's on disability and doesn't know if he'll ever be able to practice law again.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:17:03.350 line:1
I didn't think take a ride in a roller coaster at a legitimate theme park and end up with brain bleed and multiple surgeries in your head.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:04.600 line:1
And you're not the only one.

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:05.940 line:1
And I'm not the only one.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:11.620 line:1
CARTER EVANS: In 2022, 22-year-old Christopher Hawley died after riding the X2.

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:15.900 line:1
His family settled a lawsuit with the amusement park earlier this year.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.440 line:1
That's what makes me angry.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:21.760 line:1
They had been sued at least a year before I was injured.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:24.040 line:1
They knew.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:30.420 line:1
CARTER EVANS: Now, Wilk told me the force of the roller coaster slammed his head against the headrest, causing severe pain.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:32.000 line:1
That's X2 behind me.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.000 line:1
Six Flags closed it in July.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.740 line:1
And today, the company declined to comment on the pending lawsuits.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:38.380 line:1
Tony?

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:39.520 line:1
Carter, thank you.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:43.440 line:1
Coming up next, the close-call lightning strike caught on camera.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:57.120 line:1
A lightning bolt hit interstate 84 in Utah last night, the moment captured on dashcam video from a driver, who said it was louder than you can imagine.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.360 line:1
And it nearly zapped a box truck just ahead of him.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:05.900 line:1
What the driver of that truck said when it happened-- probably not safe for TV.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:12.600 line:1
A golden retriever puppy in Northern California showed off some leadership skills recently, taking this horse for a little walk.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:20.580 line:1
The horse didn't resist a bit, possibly because it thinks of itself as just a big dog, which may be what the puppy is thinking too.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:26.160 line:1
Up next, a historic home run ball and the birthday girl who appreciated it for what it was.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:38.380 line:1
We're back with the good stuff tonight, a reminder of both the innocence of children and the intended purpose of a baseball.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:47.820 line:1
It comes courtesy of this historic home run last night off the bat of a Eugenio Suárez, now just the 10th active player with 350 homers or more.

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:54.820 line:1
And somewhere out there in left field was a girl celebrating her sixth birthday, who ended up with that priceless souvenir.

00:18:54.820 --> 00:19:03.980 line:1
For a moment, it felt like the whole stadium was cheering for little Calia and probably thinking, wow, that ball has got to be worth some serious money.

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:06.380 line:1
But what does a kid care about money?

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.540 line:1
A baseball, after all, is intended to be thrown.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.980 line:1
COMMENTATOR: Eugenio Suárez's 350th career home run-- and throwing it back.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:15.040 line:1
Oh my goodness.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:18.800 line:1
CARTER EVANS: The Braves broadcast crew rushed out to see what she was thinking.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:21.200 line:1
And her answer here is just perfect.

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.620 line:1
Why did you throw it back?

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:23.640 line:1
I don't know.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.740 line:1
I just want to throw it.

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:28.480 line:1
She just wanted to throw it.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:34.040 line:1
You see, for all those cheering adults, the best part of catching a home run ball is keeping it or maybe selling it.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:41.020 line:1
But for a kid like Calia, after eight innings of watching other people play baseball, the best part was the game itself.

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:44.940 line:1
Finally, it was her turn to play.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:46.440 line:1
That's another day in America.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:47.460 line:1
I'm Tony Dokoupil.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.440 line:1
For all of us here at CBS News, thank you for watching.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:52.100 line:1
We'll see you right back here tomorrow.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:53.120 line:1
Good night.
